1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:01,360
[Music]

2
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:03,760
Hi everyone. Today we're exploring

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,080
Blitz's timer modes, the tools to track

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,759
your time and keep your productivity on

5
00:00:07,759 --> 00:00:10,400
point. Let's start with the SD countdown

6
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,759
timer. EST stands for estimated time. It

7
00:00:13,759 --> 00:00:15,440
is a great way to plan your day and give

8
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,600
yourself a target to aim for. You can

9
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,600
set the time estimate by clicking the ST

10
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,320
button next to your task. Just enter the

11
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:24,080
hours and minutes you want to dedicate

12
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,000
and press enter.

13
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,320
Alternatively, when creating a new task,

14
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:30,480
you can specify the estimate in the SD

15
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:33,120
field. A quick note, if your task is

16
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,960
already running a lifetime, meaning

17
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,480
you've started working on it in blitz

18
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,879
mode, you can only add or adjust the ST

19
00:00:38,879 --> 00:00:41,600
while the timer is on pause.

20
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,680
When the timer comes down to zero, it

21
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,079
displays a times up message. At this

22
00:00:46,079 --> 00:00:48,239
point, you can skip to the next task,

23
00:00:48,239 --> 00:00:50,559
mark it as done, or choose to extend the

24
00:00:50,559 --> 00:00:53,520
timer. If you extend, the timer keeps

25
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:55,440
running, but now it shows exactly how

26
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,079
much you are overdue. It's a friendly

27
00:00:58,079 --> 00:00:59,440
nudge that maybe your playing skills

28
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,280
could use a little sharpening. No

29
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,199
judgment here, just motivation to

30
00:01:03,199 --> 00:01:06,400
improve. Now, the Pomodoro timer.

31
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,119
Pomodoro breaks work into focus chunks,

32
00:01:09,119 --> 00:01:11,600
usually 25 minutes, followed by a short

33
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,799
break, typically 5 minutes. Why? Because

34
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:16,560
your brain deserves a rest after period

35
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:18,560
of focus work. Studies show that this

36
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:20,560
actually helps increase your stamina.

37
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:22,320
You can turn on pomodoro mode in

38
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:24,479
preferences. Customize your work

39
00:01:24,479 --> 00:01:26,880
intervals, brake length, and

40
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:29,920
notification alert sounds. When you

41
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,159
start, the timer counts down your work

42
00:01:32,159 --> 00:01:34,799
session. When it ends, you get a break

43
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:38,240
alert. Time for a quick rest. Once the

44
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:40,240
break finishes, you are notified to get

45
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,119
back to work.

46
00:01:43,119 --> 00:01:45,040
And the floating timer keeps things

47
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,520
visible but out of your way, so you stay

48
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,159
focused without losing track. If you

49
00:01:50,159 --> 00:01:51,920
haven't set any on a task and don't have

50
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,320
pomodoro turned on, then your timer will

51
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,479
count upwards and act as a basic time

52
00:01:56,479 --> 00:01:59,119
tracker, which brings us to the time

53
00:01:59,119 --> 00:02:01,439
tracking timer. Once you start the live

54
00:02:01,439 --> 00:02:03,920
timer on a task, Blitz tracks the actual

55
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,560
time you spent working on it so far. If

56
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:08,239
needed, you can also enter this time

57
00:02:08,239 --> 00:02:09,840
manually in this field using the same

58
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:12,640
input format as ST. This lets you

59
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,879
compare your original estimates with the

60
00:02:14,879 --> 00:02:16,959
real time spent, giving you a valuable

61
00:02:16,959 --> 00:02:18,720
feedback to improve your planning and

62
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,360
task management. Whether you have an ST

63
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,680
set or not, or you're using Pomodoro

64
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,760
mode, Blitz always records your real

65
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,800
time spent in the time taking field. So,

66
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:31,120
let's make a quick recap. Use ASD

67
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,120
countdown to plan your time. Use

68
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:35,200
Pomodoro to boost focus with built-in

69
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,519
breaks. And use time tracking to see how

70
00:02:37,519 --> 00:02:40,319
long tasks actually take. Try them all

71
00:02:40,319 --> 00:02:42,879
out. Your productivity will thank you.

72
00:02:42,879 --> 00:02:44,640
And don't forget to check out our other

73
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:47,040
tutorials and join our Discord community

74
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,360
for more tips and a nice group of people

75
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:51,599
passionate about getting things done.

76
00:02:51,599 --> 00:02:55,000
Happy blitzing.